// Book One, Chapter 18 (session 15). The night the Senate sits: preparation, the quarry, the judgment, and one of four resolutions.
// One night phase of three actions out of four places, then the Senate, then the choice about the General (original_fate), then the dawn. Ends with rest().
// Chapter 19 picks up the aftermath. Hunger, injury, standing and secrecy leave this chapter as the player made them.
@chapter b1c18
@title The Weight of Cedar
@book burning-city
@version 1

# start
## The night the Senate sits
$ do phase("night", 3)
$ b1.senate_ruling = "deferred"
$ b1.tarrax_after = "wary"
$ b1.seal_kind = "senate"
$ b1.general_terms = 0
$ _counting = 0
$ _talk = 0
$ _garden = 0
$ _gate = 0
$ _hold = 0
$ _terms_n = 0
$ _t_rome = 0
$ _t_name = 0
$ _t_call = 0
$ _tempted = 0
$ _asked = 0
The day goes by like a dream that somebody else is having. You sleep a little and wake and sleep again. You eat the biscuit that Hanno gave you, which you had forgotten, and which turns out to be very good. Somewhere in the house a clock of Prisca's, which she has kept in a cupboard for two centuries, is wound and set going and ticks, to the great surprise of everyone, with a sound like a very small, very patient heart.
It is dusk when you come up. The house has changed again. There are no servants in the passages. There are trunks standing in the atrium, corded and sealed, with the six petalled flower on their lids, and Hanno is going from one to another with a taper, checking the seals as a man tests the ice.
?if must_feed()
  And you are hungry. You have been hungry for some time, and it has been quiet, as it is before a storm, and it has found you in the atrium in the lamplight with the whole of a crowded house about you and the smell of blood in every wall.
  * Go to the stables, and take a mare, and be quick about it
    $ do feed("animal")
    $ humanity += 1
    It is not what the hunger wanted. It is enough.
  * Go to the donors behind the garden, and pay ?if wealth >= 3
    $ do feed("donor")
    $ do earn(-3)
    $ humanity -= 1
    A woman in a plain stola holds out her arm without looking at you, as at a barber's. It is clean. It is quick. It is very tidy.
  * Go on, as you are
    $ cunning_brutal += 2
    $ _asked += 1
    You go on hungry. It sharpens things. The house is full of beating hearts, and you walk through them with your hands in your sleeves.
?end
The Senate sits at the second watch. You have the first watch, and a part of the second, and three things that you can do with it.
-> night

# night
## The first watch
:night
?if actions_left() == 3
  The sun is not long gone. A cold small wind goes round the courtyard, and the fig tree in the garden has begun, very faintly, to rattle.
?elif actions_left() == 2
  It is full dark. The lamps in the atrium have been lit, and a great many people are in the lane outside, and nobody is speaking.
?else
  The clock in the cupboard has struck. It is nearly the hour. You can hear the grey line forming in the lane, and the sound of cedar on stone, very faint, from the direction of the Esquiline.
?end
* Go to Hanno, in the counting room [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> counting
  $ do act()
* Go down to the strongroom, for the last talk [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> talk
  $ do act()
* Go out into the garden, where someone is waiting [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> garden
  $ do act()
* Go to the gate, and the Scribe line [once] ?if actions_left() > 0
  ~> gate
  $ do act()
?if actions_left() <= 0
  $ do end_phase()
  -> senate
?end
-> :night

# counting
## The counting room
$ _counting += 1
$ do meet("hanno")
Hanno has put a table in the middle of the counting room, and nothing on it but a lamp, and a tablet, and a very small bag. He has put on a clean tunic. It is a thing that you have never seen him do, and it makes him look, quite suddenly, extremely old.
"The lady says that you may have anything in the house," he says, in the voice of a man who has been rehearsing it. "I am to tell you what the house has. I am to be accurate. The lady was very clear about that."
:counting_ask
* Ask what the house has in cedar [once]
  He looks at you for a moment without moving. "There is a cell," says Hanno. "It is not large. The lady laid it down in the cellars eighty years ago, in a year when she was afraid of something, and it has been in a crate under the wine, in pieces, with a book of plans." He lets out a breath. "It would hold a man. It would not hold *him* for ever. It would hold him for as long as the person who held the key were alive to hold it." The tablet has a number on it. "It is twelve."
  * Have it brought out, and pay ?if wealth >= 12
    $ do earn(-12)
    $ flag b1.cedar_cell
    "Yes," says Hanno, who has gone slightly pale. "I thought that it might be." He makes a mark in the ledger. It is a very small mark, and it will be there when the house is gone.
  * Leave it in its crate
    "Of course," says Hanno. His face does not change in any particular.
  -> :counting_ask
* Ask what the house has in the way of a road [once]
  "There is a boat at the Emporium," says Hanno. "It is a river boat, and a plain one, with no flower on it. There is a man at Ostia who owes the lady a favour and does not ask what it was for. There is a pair of horses at the Porta Ostiensis, and a courier who has done this before." He considers. "It would carry one person who was easy to carry. Or a great many who were not, if you had a day to arrange it. It is ten."
  * Have the road made ready, and pay ?if wealth >= 10
    $ do earn(-10)
    $ flag b1.escort
    "Yes," says Hanno, with the faintest suggestion of a bow. "The lady will be glad to have been asked." He writes it down. It is a very small mark.
  * Leave it
    "Of course," says Hanno.
  -> :counting_ask
* Ask what he thinks will happen [once]
  Hanno is quiet for such a long time that you suppose he has not heard. Then he says, gravely, as a man gives up a coin: "I have kept the books of this house for three hundred years. I have known six Commanders of the Lictors, two Firsts of the Senate, and a good many people who were afraid." He looks at the lamp. "I have never kept a book that had *him* in it. I find that I would like to see what the entries are."
  It is, you understand, the nearest thing to a prediction that he has ever made.
  -> :counting_ask
* That is enough. Take your leave
  "Go with God," says Hanno, who learned it from the clerk, "or with whomever you are going with."
  ~> counting_out
  <~
-> :counting_ask

# counting_out
You go out and the door shuts, softly, on the lamp and the tablet and the very small bag. Somebody in the house is singing under their breath, a plain, rough, unmusical tune that you almost know.
<~

# talk
## The last talk
$ _talk += 1
$ do meet("general")
They have taken the guards from the door. It is a courtesy. The General is at the table when you come in, with the borrowed tunic and a plain grey cloak on top of it, and he is looking at the dark brazier with the air of a man who has been asked to make a decision about a horse.
He is a good deal better. There is a faint warmth in him now, a gloss on the old parchment, and he is able to stand. He does not. He seems to be keeping it for the quarry.
"Sit," he says. "There is wine. It is dreadful. It is the only thing in the house that Prisca did not think of being generous about."
:talk_ask
* Stand and look at him for a long time [once]
  You do not sit. You stand across the table with your hands at your sides and look at him, and you find that the hunger has taken an interest. It is not a thing that you asked it to do. It has gone very quiet, and very sharp, and very still, and the room has begun to smell of him, of dust and iron and old oil and under both of them something dark, and enormous, and warm.
  He sees it in your face. He does not move. He says, in a mild voice, "It would be a great deal of me."
  You say nothing.
  "I should not enjoy it," says the General, with enormous calm. "I would not forgive it. I say so not as a threat. I say so as a matter of record, because there are a great many people who would like to know." He sips the wine, and makes a face. "I should be a very bad enemy. I should have time to be one."
  $ _tempted += 1
  $ hunger += 4
  -> :talk_ask
* Ask what he will do if he is let go [once]
  "I should like," says the General slowly, "to go and sit by a river for a week." He is looking at the wall. "I have a notion that it is a thing that I used to do. I should like to find out whether I liked it. After that, I should like to be told what has become of my people, in detail, by someone who was not trying to flatter me." He smiles. "I do not say that I should not do the other things. I say that I should begin with the river."
  $ bond.general += 1
  $ _asked += 1
  -> :talk_ask
* Ask what happens to him if he is put back [once]
  He is silent. It is a long silence, and Meritamun's lamp is not in the room to fill it. "It depends what they put me back *with*," says the General at last. He picks up the cup and puts it down. "A seal is a sort of opinion. It lasts as long as everyone agrees with it. Cedar from a grove that was planted for the purpose will hold a long time. Cedar from a crate will hold until somebody is frightened enough to knock."
  He looks at you. There is nothing in the look that is hard to bear except the kindness.
  "I will go in, if it is put to me. I said that I would abide. I wish you would not put it to me."
  $ bond.general += 1
  $ _asked += 1
  -> :talk_ask
* Ask for his word that he will not feed on Rome while you live [once]
  "That is a great deal to ask of a hungry man," says the General. But he is smiling, a little, and he does not say no. "I will think about it. I will think about it in a way that you may rely on." He lifts one finger. "I should like it understood that I do not promise to *like* it."
  $ _t_rome += 1
  $ _terms_n += 1
  -> :talk_ask
* Ask for his true name [once] ?if bond.general >= 2
  The smile goes. It is replaced by a look that has in it no more than a very slight, very formal surprise, as of a man who has been asked for his house keys by someone he had been about to give them to.
  "It is not a thing that I have said aloud," says the General, "since the second year of the plague." He looks at you. He seems to be doing a sum. It takes some time. "It is a very small thing to give, and the largest that I have. I will say it once, and you will not need to hear it twice."
  He says it. It is very short. It is a sound that you would not have thought a human mouth could make, and when he has finished you find that you can say it back, and that you will be able to say it back for as long as you live.
  $ do learn_name("general")
  $ _t_name += 1
  $ _terms_n += 1
  $ bond.general += 2
  -> :talk_ask
* Ask him to come when you call, if you ever do [once]
  "Ah," says the General. He seems genuinely touched. "That is the nicest thing that I have been asked." He puts his big hand flat on the table. "I will come. I do not say that I will come quickly, or in a good temper. I do not say that I will not tell you what I think of the summons." And then, in the voice of a man who has remembered that he is old: "I would come in any case. But it is pleasant to be asked."
  $ _t_call += 1
  $ _terms_n += 1
  $ bond.general += 1
  -> :talk_ask
* That is enough. Take your leave
  "Go and be clever," says the General. "I shall sit here and be a nuisance to Hanno."
  ~> talk_out
  <~
-> :talk_ask

# talk_out
The oak door shuts, and the bolt is not shot. It is a courtesy that you notice more than you ought to.
<~

# garden
## Under the fig
$ _garden += 1
$ do meet("tarrax")
It is cold in the garden. There is no moon, and the sky is a very deep and starry blue, and the fig tree stands with its leaves half turned, silvered and dim, like a ship in a harbour. There is a man on the stone bench beneath it.
He has come alone. It is a thing that you can tell at once. There are no Lictors in the lane, no red cloaks at the wall. He is wearing a plain dark soldier's cloak without a mark upon it, and his hands are on his knees, and he has the stillness of a man who has been sitting there for some hours and would be perfectly content to go on.
"Sit," says Tarrax. "It is a good tree. I have known it since it was a twig."
:garden_ask
* Ask him what he came for [once]
  "To ask a thing," says Tarrax. "And to say that I will not be able to ask it tomorrow, in front of them, in the manner that I would like." He looks at the fig. "I want him sealed. I do not say that I want him *hurt*. I do not say that I have ever wanted that. I say that if he walks out of the quarry tomorrow, every line in this city will follow him, and not us, and there is a great deal of Rome which is asleep tonight on the supposition that there are rules."
  $ _garden += 1
  -> :garden_ask
* Ask him what he feared in the quarry [once]
  He is quiet for a long time. When he speaks, he speaks to the tree. "I was told when I was made," says Tarrax, "that I was of the first line, and that the first line had a father. I was not told that he was alive. I was told that he had gone to sleep." He turns his hands over, and looks at the palms. "I have been the Commander of the Lictors for a great many years. I have done what the Senate has asked. It had not occurred to me that I might one day kneel for no reason that I could name."
  $ bond.tarrax += 1
  -> :garden_ask
* Ask him whether the cell is as the Senate says [once]
  "It is sixty beams," says Tarrax. "I laid the first of them myself. I have not looked at it for some time." He pauses. "It is a plain room, with a floor of clay and a window the width of a hand that looks out on a wall. It has been prepared for a person who was to be comfortable. I made certain of that. I should like you to know it."
  He looks, for just a moment, as though he would like to say something else. He does not.
  $ _garden += 1
  -> :garden_ask
* Ask what he will do if the General is let go [once]
  "I shall be very sorry," says Tarrax. He says it with no emphasis. "It is what the Senate has always done. It will be sorry, afterward, and it will have a great many documents about it." He turns his head, and in the starlight his face is the colour of a bone. "I will do what I am told. I want you to know that I will do it exactly. I will not be glad."
  $ bond.tarrax -= 1
  -> :garden_ask
* Tell him that you will promise nothing [once]
  "Of course not," says Tarrax. He seems almost relieved. "I should think less of you." He rises, with the unhurried courtesy of a man who has been in a great many rooms. "Galerius says that you gave him a name. A name is a thing that I notice." He lets that sit between you. "I will not forget it."
  ?if b1.tarrax_answer == "truth"
    He says, with great care, "You told me, in the quarry, who made you. I did not say what I thought of it. I should like you to know that I thought a great deal."
  ?elif b1.tarrax_answer == "lie"
    He says, with great care, "You told me, in the quarry, who made you. It was not the truth. I have not said so. I should like you to know that I know it."
    $ bond.tarrax -= 1
  ?end
  $ bond.tarrax += 1
  ~> garden_out
  <~
* That is enough. Take your leave
  "Go and be clever," says Tarrax. It is the General's phrase, and he does not seem to have noticed that it is.
  ~> garden_out
  <~
-> :garden_ask

# garden_out
You leave him under the tree. When you look back from the door, he is sitting exactly as he was, with his hands on his knees, a tall grey shape against the dark, and you cannot tell whether he is waiting or has forgotten that you went.
<~

# gate
## The Scribe line and the grey
$ _gate += 1
$ do meet("meritamun")
$ do meet("theron")
The lane has filled. They stand shoulder to shoulder in the dark, in plain grey, with their hoods back and their hands empty, a hundred of them at least, and not one of them is looking at the house. They are looking at the sky, and at the street, and at the place at the end of it where the Esquiline lifts black against the stars.
Meritamun is sitting in the gate on a camp stool, with her table folded and her lamp at her feet, and a scroll across her knees that she is not reading. Theron is standing at her shoulder.
"I have been asked to witness something," says Meritamun without preamble. She has not looked up. "By nobody. I have been thinking about it since the quarry, and I find that I have a stylus and nothing in the way of a conscience to stand between us."
:gate_ask
* Ask her what a witness does [once]
  "In the old law," says Meritamun, "a bargain with one of the Six is not a private matter. It was not intended to be. It is a thing that is done before three heads of houses, who say aloud that they have heard it, and who bind themselves to say so afterward to anyone who should ask. It makes it a thing that cannot be called a lie." She lets a little silence fall. "It does not make it a *good* bargain. It makes it a lawful one, and in the Senate's eyes, that has sometimes been the greater difficulty."
  $ _gate += 1
  -> :gate_ask
* Ask her to witness it, if there is a bargain [once]
  "Ah," says Meritamun. She seems quite undismayed. "I thought that you would." She turns the scroll over. "I will stand. I will bring Prisca, who will be too frightened to refuse, and I will bring Theron, who will not be." She glances up. "It will cost me something with the Senate. I would like it recorded that I considered that, and that I do not much mind."
  $ flag b1.witnessed
  $ bond.meritamun += 2
  -> :gate_ask
* Ask Theron to hold the mouth of the quarry, if it comes to it [once]
  "It will be done," says Theron, instantly. His voice is hoarse. "If you give me a sign. I would be very glad of a sign." He looks at the grey ranks in the lane, and at you. "I will not do it without one. I would like it to be understood that I am not a man who disobeys. I am a man who has not been told what to do since Macedon."
  $ _hold += 1
  $ bond.theron += 1
  -> :gate_ask
* Tell Theron that the line is to stand down whatever happens [once]
  He does not answer for a long time. His jaw works. He says, "Very well," in a voice like cracked pottery. It is the voice of a man who has been told by a person he is trying to love that he must keep his hands in his sleeves.
  $ bond.theron -= 2
  $ _hold -= 1
  -> :gate_ask
* That is enough. Take your leave
  "It is a very short way to the quarry," says Meritamun. She picks up her lamp. "It is a very long one to come back."
  ~> gate_out
  <~
-> :gate_ask

# gate_out
The grey line parts to let you through, and closes behind you, and nobody touches you. It is a thing that you will remember for some time: the hundred of them, and the silence, and the faint rustle that a great number of hoods make when a great number of heads are turned together.
<~

# senate
## The old quarry, at the second watch
The old quarry has been given back its torches. They are black iron brands at forty paces, and they burn with a still red light that does not flicker, and the quarry floor is bright with them from end to end.
It is full. The lines stand in their order, by house and by age, as they have stood for longer than anyone remembers, and between them, on the swept pale stone, there is a long clear aisle that runs from the mouth of the path to the open door of green bronze. The door stands wide. The black stair goes down behind it, quite empty, and nobody looks at it.
At the head of the aisle there is a figure on a plain stool. It is hooded, in red wool, with the face entirely in shadow. It does not stir. It has no name that anyone has ever used. It is called the First of the Senate, and it has presided in this place, in this chair, for longer than the walls have been cut.
Tarrax stands at its right hand. He has put on the black iron cuirass. It makes him look like a man made of the dark. Galerius is two paces behind him, and he does not lift his eyes.
?if b1.door_side == "theron"
  The General's line is at the left of the aisle, in a long grey rank, and it is bigger than you remembered. It has brought no arms. It has brought a great many faces.
?elif b1.door_side == "tarrax"
  The Lictors are at the right of the aisle in their red files, with the cedar grounded, and they have the look of men who are about to be proved right and are not at all looking forward to it.
?else
  They are on either side of the aisle, the grey and the red, and you walk down the middle of it with your hands empty, as you did on the night of the door, and nobody pretends that the position has a name.
?end
Prisca is at the foot of the aisle, with Hanno beside her. She is quite white. She does not look at you. She is holding the abacus against her chest as a child might hold a doll.
Meritamun stands at the edge of the lines with her table, her lamp and her reed. She has not got a witness's cloak. She has brought, instead, a very plain piece of white linen, and she is holding it folded over her arm.
Then there is a stir at the mouth of the path, and the quarry draws its breath.
He comes down the aisle on his own feet. He has put on a plain grey cloak. It is not a very good one, and it does not fit, and it has been fastened at the throat by a pin from Prisca's own cloak, a small bronze flower, six petalled. He walks slowly, with a hand on nobody, with his head up and his great ruined shoulders squared. He stops in the middle of the aisle, where the torches are brightest, and he looks round him, unhurried, at the whole of the quarry, and the lines, and the red wool, and the open door.
"Good evening," says the General. "I regret that I have not got a hat."
Nobody laughs. It is a thing that you feel go through the whole of the quarry like a wind through grass, the effort of three hundred people not to.
The First does not move. A voice comes out of the hood, thin, flat, courteous, without any age at all. "The Senate of Blood sits to hear the matter of the guest of the Merchant house. Who is his surety?"
?if b1.general_meal == "none"
  Prisca's hand tightens on the beads. She steps forward, a pace, with her chin up. "I am," she says. Her voice shakes, and she does not allow it to stop her. "I gave him no drink, but it was given in my house, by my steward, at my word."
  The General has turned his head. "Madam," he says, with a great and unexpected gentleness, "you are mistaken. You are not. I have *chosen*." He looks round, a little gravely, at the Senate. "By the old custom a ward may, at the quarry, before sentence, name another in his surety's place, if the other will stand. I name him." And he points, with one huge, bony finger, and with an air of faint apology, at you.
  Meritamun says, from the edge of the lines, in a dry, clear voice, "It is in the third roll. I wrote it. Some of you were there." There is a dreadful pause. Nobody asks which of them.
?else
  You step forward. It is not a thing that you decided to do. You find that you have done it, and that you are standing in the aisle, a pace in front of the lines, with the whole of the quarry looking at the back of your head.
  "He is mine," you say. It is not a thing that you meant to say, in quite that way.
  There is a movement along the ranks. The red wool does not shift. "By blood, or by hand?" says the First.
  ?if b1.general_meal == "wrist"
    "By blood," you say.
  ?elif b1.general_meal == "donor"
    "By hand," you say. "I brought him a man, and he asked his name."
  ?else
    You say, a little desperately, "By courtesy. I brought him a lamb."
    There is a silence. And then somewhere in the grey ranks a man begins, helplessly, to cough. It is not a cough, and it is not a laugh, and it is a thing that one could be put to death for.
  ?end
?end
"Then he is yours to answer for," says the First. It is said without either blessing or threat. It is merely a fact being entered in a book. "What does the guest say?"
The General looks at you.
He looks for a long time. There is nothing in the look that you could call a request. It is the look of a man who has laid a very great weight on someone else's shoulders and does not intend to pretend that it is not a weight.
"I will abide," says the General, to the quarry, to the stars. "For one night. By what he says."
-> hearing

# hearing
## What they asked
They speak, one after another. It is the form, and the form is very old, and it takes a long time, and you stand through the whole of it with the torchlight on the side of your face.
Tarrax speaks first, and he says in a level voice that the Senate is the keeper of the peace of the dead, and that the peace has depended for two thousand years on certain things not being done. He does not use the General's name. He says, "That which lay beneath the hill." He says that it is a sorrow, and that the Senate regrets it, and that the Senate has prepared a place. He does not look at the General. He looks at a point at the level of your collarbone, with the fixed, unshaken, absolute concentration of a man looking at a wall.
Theron speaks next. He does not stand. He kneels, on the stone, with the ruined ear and the scarred face and the cracked pottery voice, and he says that the General's line has kept the door for longer than the Senate has had a chair, and that it asks for nothing but its father. He says, "Let him come home." It is not an argument. It is the whole of what he has.
Prisca speaks, a few words. She says that the Merchant house asks only to be left out of the matter. She says it like a woman signing her own death warrant, and she does not lower her eyes.
Meritamun speaks. She says that the Scribe line has recorded the whole of the night of the door and the three nights since. She says that it will record this. She says, with a sudden spark of something that is very nearly joy, "I would remind the Senate that it has never yet been in a position to say that it has been *present*."
And then there is nobody left. The First's hood turns, very slightly, toward you.
?if b1.door_side == "theron"
  "The one who stood with the line," says the First, without inflection, "will speak."
?elif b1.door_side == "tarrax"
  "The one who stood with the Commander," says the First, without inflection, "will speak."
?elif b1.door_side == "alone"
  "The one who went first to the door," says the First, without inflection, "will speak."
?else
  "The one who stood between," says the First, without inflection, "will speak."
?end
?if bond.general < 0
  The General is looking at the torches. He has the stillness of a man who has been disappointed in a friend and has decided not to say so.
?end
There is a great quiet in the quarry. The torches burn without a sound. Somewhere far above, a night bird is calling, over and over, a single plain note.
[roman] It is a Roman thing, to be asked to speak before the Senate. You have been told since you could walk that it is the greatest honour a citizen can be given, and it has not occurred to you until this moment that it would feel so exactly like being on a roof.
[not roman] It is not your Senate. You have always known that, and you have been very careful to know it, and it seems that it makes no difference whatever.
You have the General, and the Senate, and the dark. You have four things that you might do.
:judgment
* Let him go
  -> loosed
* Make terms with him, before witnesses
  -> bargain
* Ask the First for the old right of judgment, an hour alone with him, and take what is in him
  -> drunk
* Give him to the cedar
  -> sealed
-> :judgment

# loosed
## Let him go
"He goes free," you say.
It is not loud. It does not need to be. It goes out across the quarry like a stone dropped in a well, and you hear it strike the walls and come back, and everything in the quarry draws itself in.
"He is the guest of no one, and the prisoner of no one," you say, and your voice sounds odd in your own ears, thin and high and extremely clear. "He has been under the hill for longer than there has been a city on it. I will not put him under a roof. I will not put him back."
The General does not move. But you see his hand come up, slowly, to the pin at his throat, and rest there, as a man's might rest on a doorframe.
There is a long silence. Then the First says, in a voice that has not changed at all, "The Senate hears." It is a statement without any form of agreement in it. "Commander."
Tarrax has not stirred. He has turned his head, very slightly, in your direction. There is nothing in his face. It is the face of a man who has a great deal of experience of very bad news and is not going to be hurried by this one. He says, in a level voice, "The Lictors will close the mouth of the quarry."
It is the order that he was always going to give. It is given a little late.
?if _hold > 0
  Theron is on his feet. You did not see him rise. He lifts his ruined hand, and behind him the whole of the grey rank rises, like a tide coming in, with no sound but the whisper of cloth, and goes up the aisle between the torches, and is simply *there*, at the mouth of the path, a hundred plain grey figures with their hands empty and the red files of the Lictors drawn up before them. Nobody has drawn a weapon. Nobody is going to be the first.
  The General walks up the aisle between them, on his own feet, slowly, with his head back.
  Tarrax watches him go. He does not give the second order. It is the whole of the order that he does not give, and it hangs in the air over the quarry, and you see his jaw tighten, once, and the hand on his sword go white, and open.
  The Lictors do not move. They have heard it, as well, in the silence. They part.
?else
  The Lictors move. They are very fast. They come across the swept stone in their files with their cedar levelled, and it is not a charge, it is a closing of doors, and they are between the General and the mouth of the path before he has gone a dozen paces.
  You are in front of him. You are not quite sure how.
  ~> loose_fight
?end
He stops at the mouth of the quarry. He turns, for a moment, in the night wind, with the whole of the red torchlight on one side of his face and the stars on the other.
"I should like it understood," says the General, to the whole of the Senate, "that I am not angry. I am only extremely interested." And then, to you, in a lower voice, with a faint, creaking, helpless trace of the laugh: "I owe you a river, little one. I am a very bad debtor. You will find me a great trial."
?if b1.escort
  The courier from Ostia is at the mouth of the path with two horses and a cloak with no flower on it. The General looks at them. He says, with deep gravity, "I have never ridden a horse that did not have an opinion." He mounts, and goes out into the dark, with Theron running at his stirrup, with the whole of the grey line streaming up the road behind him like a cloak, and a very small bronze flower catching the torchlight at his throat.
?else
  Theron is at his elbow. He does not speak. He has put his arm round the General's back, in the dark, in the cold, like a son, and the General puts a hand on his shoulder and leans on it with enormous care, and so they go out, with the grey line closing round them, toward a house on the Aventine that has had a bed made up in it for a hundred and ninety years.
?end
