# loose_fight
## The mouth of the quarry
$ do fight("lictor", 2)
:loose_exchange
* Take the front man's stake, and break it
  $ do strike("brutal")
* Go under it, and into the file
  $ do strike("cunning")
* Say, loud, that the Senate has heard him, and that the Senate does not strike a guest
  $ do strike("talk")
?if not fight_over()
  -> :loose_exchange
?end
?if fight_result() == "talked"
  $ standing += 1
  There is a ragged moment. A Lictor at the end of the file says, quite clearly, "That is true." Another says, "It is the old law." Nobody orders them to stop. They stop.
?elif fight_result() == "won"
  $ injury += 2
  The front rank breaks. It is not a rout. It is only that the Lictors, who are not used to being refused, have not planned for it, and that the stakes go down like a field of reeds.
?else
  $ injury += 6
  $ hunger += 6
  You are knocked flat. You are on the swept stone with a red boot on your ribs and the sound of cedar cracking, and you do not see how he does it. You only hear a voice above you say, with immense, cheerful weariness, "Oh, *do* get up," and the boot goes away.
?end
The General is standing over you with one huge hand open. It is not a very great hand now. It is shaking. It has in it, nevertheless, a stillness that the Lictors have felt, and that they have not got a word for, and the red files have fallen back, one by one, like a hedge from a plough.
<~

# bargain
## Terms
"He goes free," you say, "on terms."
There is a stir along the ranks. "Terms," says the First. It is the first thing that it has said with any trace of color, and it is not a pleasant color. "A bargain is a thing that is made between equals."
"Then I should be obliged," says the General, in a mild voice, "if somebody would tell the Senate that I am not, at present, in a position to be anything else."
You do not look at the General. You look at the red hood, and you say, "I will name them before witnesses." You say it to the torches. "I have asked Meritamun of the Scribe line to hear it. I have asked nobody else."
It is the first time that you have said aloud that you have something to give.
:terms
?if _terms_n >= 3
  -> terms_done
?end
* Ask for his word that he will not feed on Rome while you live ?if _t_rome == 0
  $ _t_rome += 1
  $ _terms_n += 1
  "He will not take what is not offered in this city," you say, "for as long as I live." There is a sound from the Lictors, a kind of breathing, that is the nearest thing a rank of vampires can come to a cheer.
  "I give it," says the General. It is entirely without irony. "I shall be extremely hungry, and I give it."
* Ask for his true name ?if bond.general >= 2 and _t_name == 0
  $ _t_name += 1
  $ _terms_n += 1
  $ do learn_name("general")
  He looks at you. It is a quick, shrewd, curious look, and then a smile that has in it something almost tender, and he bends his head, as to a king, and says it, so softly that nobody but you hears. It is a very short word.
  Nobody in the quarry knows what you have been told. Everyone in it knows that you have been told.
* Ask him to come when you call ?if _t_call == 0
  $ _t_call += 1
  $ _terms_n += 1
  "He will come when I send for him," you say. There is an awful, silent, appalled attention along the ranks. "I do not say that it will be quickly."
  "Nor in a good temper," says the General, bowing.
* Say that it is enough ?if _terms_n >= 1
  -> terms_done
-> :terms

# terms_done
## Witnessed
?if b1.witnessed
  Meritamun steps out onto the swept stone. She has the white linen over her arm and she shakes it out as she walks. It is a cloth, a plain one, hemmed in black, and she lays it across the aisle between you and the General, and says in the old speech, quite fast, in the exact flat voice in which she gives the knock count, a formula that is three thousand years old.
  Prisca comes forward. She is shaking in every limb. She puts her hand on the linen. Theron, on his knees, puts his hand beside hers. It is a ragged thing, a thing done a little late, and it is binding.
  "It is witnessed," says Meritamun. "I will record it. It will not be possible, hereafter, for anyone in this quarry to say that it was not heard." She lifts her eyes, for one fleeting instant, to the red hood. "It is, you will find, entirely lawful."
  The First does not answer for a long time. The torches burn. It is the silence of an immense machine, discovering that it has no part for what is being asked of it.
  "The Senate," it says at last, in the same flat voice, "defers."
  $ b1.senate_ruling = "deferred"
  $ standing += 1
  $ bond.meritamun += 1
?else
  You look round, and there is nobody. Meritamun is at the edge of the lines with her lamp, and her face is very still, and she has not been asked. You have not asked her to come forward. It is, you realise, a thing that you should have done before you came.
  "A bargain," says the First, into the silence, "must be witnessed. It has not been. The Senate does not find a bargain." It is said with perfect politeness. "It finds a defiance."
  There is a long moment in which the quarry does not breathe. Then the General laughs. It is a rusty, creaking, helpless sound, and it rolls round the walls like a barrel. "My dear sir," he says to the First, "I have been *called* a great many things. It is the first time that I have been called a defiance. I am extremely flattered."
  Nobody moves. Nobody dares to be the first to. And after a very long time, it is Tarrax, in his black iron, who turns, and gives a low, level order to the files, and they ground their cedar and fall back, and are seen not to look at anyone.
  $ b1.senate_ruling = "defied"
?end
$ b1.general_terms = _terms_n
$ do original_fate("general", "bargain")
$ b1.tarrax_after = "wary"
$ bond.general += 2
$ bond.tarrax -= 1
The General turns, in the torchlight, to look at you. He has, you see, no more colour in his face than a man who has been standing for an hour, and a very great deal in his eyes.
"It is a thing that I did not expect," he says in a low voice, "to find that I would be glad of a leash." He settles the borrowed cloak. "It is very light. I had been afraid that it would be heavy."
He goes out between the red files with a plain grey rank closing round him, and nobody touches him, and he does not look back.
