# drunk
## The hour of judgment
You say it quite quietly. It is, you find, a thing that your mouth knows how to say. "I ask the old right."
The First lifts its hood. It is the first time that it has done so. It does not show you a face. It shows you, for a breath, the dark of the red wool, and in it, very far back, two small points of light, and a sense of an enormous, tired, old attention that is turned on you like a lamp.
"The surety may judge his ward in private, before sentence," it says. "It is a right. It is seldom asked. It has not been asked for four hundred years." The light in the hood goes steady. "It is understood that what the surety does in the hour is his own. The Senate does not ask. The Senate does not hear."
There is a sound from the grey ranks. It is only Theron, a kind of broken indrawn breath, and the sound of a hundred people not moving.
The General has turned his head. He looks at you for a moment with something that is almost interest.
"Ah," he says, quite softly, in the voice of a man who has been told a joke that he saw coming. "Down there, I suppose. It is where I am used to being robbed."
He goes, of his own accord, to the green door, and down the black stair, one hand on the wall, slowly, with a very straight back. You go after him. Nobody has ordered it. Nobody speaks. The torches go out of your sight one by one, and there is a cold, dry, close dark, and the smell of dust and iron and old oil, and then there is nothing at all but the two of you, and a long, low, shadowed room with a floor of black stone, where a very big man has sat down on the bottom step with his hands on his knees.
He looks up at you. His face is perfectly calm.
"I did say," says the General, "that I should be a very bad enemy."
:drunk_do
* Take it. All of it
  -> drunk_take
* Take only a little, and step back
  -> drunk_little
-> drunk_out

# drunk_take
$ _tempted += 2
$ age += 1
$ humanity -= 6
$ cunning_brutal += 8
$ hunger -= 40
You do not remember crossing the floor.
He does not struggle. He has said that he would not. It is a thing that you will be very sorry, later, to have heard. There is only the long cold dark, and a great hand that comes up, once, and closes on your sleeve, and holds it as one holds the hand of someone who is being sick. And then there is the taste.
It is not blood. It is a flood. It goes through you like a river at the break of the ice, and you are on your knees, and a thousand years go by, a thousand nights, a thousand faces coming up out of a dark like the bottoms of ponds, and you are, for the length of a heartbeat, a man lying on a stone floor among a great many others, dying of something, with the sound of coughing all round you and, somewhere, a woman singing.
It stops. It does not stop. It goes on in you, in your bones, in the roots of your hair, a kind of huge, golden, unbearable ease. You have not been so well in all your life. You are, you realise, quite as strong as he was, and a good deal stronger than you were an hour ago, and you are kneeling on a cold stone floor in the dark with your hands in his cloak.
He is lying against the wall. He is grey. He is breathing, slow and shallow, like a man in a fever, and there is a look in his face that you cannot meet.
"Well," says the General, in a voice like a rope that has been pulled through a pulley. "That is that."
-> drunk_out

# drunk_little
$ _tempted += 1
$ age += 1
$ humanity -= 3
$ cunning_brutal += 4
$ hunger -= 25
It is a very careful thing. You do it as a man might lift a lid. You take one swallow, and the dark takes you a good deal further than you had planned, and for a long moment you are on a stone floor among a great many others, dying of something, with a woman singing. It goes through you as a cold wind goes through a house.
You stop. It is not at all easy. It is the hardest thing you have ever done, and you do it, and you sit back on your heels with your hands shaking, and the General lies against the wall, grey, breathing, looking at the roof.
"A little," he says, in a thread of a voice. "It is generous. I shall remember that it was a little."
-> drunk_out

# drunk_out
?if b1.general_meal == "wrist"
  He turns his head to look at you. It takes a long time. "I said once that I would try to be worth it," he says. "I think that I was. I should like you to remember that, as well."
?end
$ bond.general = -60
"I will remember the taste of that," says the General. He closes his eyes. "I should not like you to suppose that I will not." His voice is almost kind. "It will take me some time. I have a great deal."
You go up the stair alone. It is a long climb. It is a good deal easier than you remember.
When you come out into the torchlight the whole of the quarry is looking at you. They have seen you go down. They see you come up. You can feel what they see, in the way their faces change, a sort of ripple across the ranks, a kind of recoil. You are brighter. You are not very much, and it is a thing that nobody would be able to say, but it is in the set of your shoulders and in the way the torches lie on you, and it is not a thing that can be taken back.
Tarrax has gone, quite visibly, the colour of old ivory. It is the face of a man who has just watched the thing that he has dreaded for two thousand years happen to somebody else. He says nothing. He does not need to.
"He will not trouble you," you say to the First. You hear it, and you do not recognise the voice.
There is a very long silence.
The grey ranks come down the aisle, with Theron in front, and nobody says a word. They go down the black stair, and they come up again, very slowly, carrying him on a cloak, as men carry a king who has been wounded in a battle that he did not choose. Theron's face is wet. It is quite without expression. He does not look at you at all.
"The Senate," says the First, at last, "has not heard." It is the only thing that it can find to say, and it says it in the same flat voice, and it is not at all steady.
