# sealed
## The cedar
"Put him back," you say.
You say it so quietly that for a moment you are not sure that you have said it. It falls into the great silence of the quarry like a coin into a well. And then you hear a sound, from the left of the aisle, which is not a cry, which is a kind of terrible deep indrawn breath, and which goes on for a long time, and is Theron.
The General does not move. He stands with his hand on the little bronze pin at his throat. He looks at you quite gently, with the whole of that great, tired, kind attention, and he says in a low voice, "I said that I would abide." He nods. "I should like it noted that I am not angry. I should like it noted that I shall remember that I was not."
Nobody in the quarry says anything. There is nothing in the world that anyone could say.
?if b1.cedar_cell
  There are two ways to do it, and you have been given both. There is the Senate's cell, sixty beams, in the cellars of the Senate house, under the Commander's hand. And there is a crate under the wine in the Merchant house, with a book of plans, that holds a man for as long as the one who has the key is alive to hold it.
  * Give him to the Senate's cell
    $ b1.seal_kind = "senate"
  * Seal him in the Merchant house's cell, and keep the key yourself
    $ b1.seal_kind = "yours"
?elif b1.cedar
  There are two ways to do it, and the Senate's cell is only one. The other is in your sleeve: nine small pegs of cedar from the lintel of the door, pale and light and cold in your hand. They would hold a man. They would hold him as long as somebody had not yet been frightened enough to knock.
  * Give him to the Senate's cell
    $ b1.seal_kind = "senate"
  * Use the nine pegs
    $ b1.seal_kind = "thin"
?else
  There is only one way to do it. The Senate has a cell, sixty beams, in the cellars of the Senate house, under the Commander's hand, and nothing else in the whole of Rome that would hold him.
  $ b1.seal_kind = "senate"
?end
?if b1.seal_kind == "senate"
  Galerius comes forward with four of the Lictors. He does not lift his eyes. He is very careful, and very gentle, and he does not touch the General, who walks between them down the long aisle with his head up, as a man might go in to dinner. The Commander walks behind. He does not give an order. He has, you see, no order to give.
  Tarrax goes with him as far as the door of the Senate house, you are told, and sees the cedar shut, and the iron straps drawn, and puts his own hand on the lock. He is a long time. When he comes out he has not got his sword, and nobody asks where he left it.
  $ b1.senate_ruling = "accepted"
  $ b1.tarrax_after = "satisfied"
  $ standing += 3
  $ bond.tarrax += 3
  $ velati_regard += 2
?elif b1.seal_kind == "yours"
  Hanno has the crate brought up on a cart at the second hour, and it is put together, in front of the whole of the Senate, on the swept stone floor of the quarry, by six clerks of the Merchant house and a carpenter who has been paid not to say what it is for. It is a plain cell. It has no window. It is not very large. When it is finished, the General walks into it of his own accord, and sits down on the floor, and folds his hands, and looks up at you through the open door with an expression of enormous, dry amusement.
  "It is a good deal more comfortable than the last," he says. "I should like it understood that I had it in mind."
  You lock it. There is a key. It is a very small, plain, iron key, and it is warm from your hand, and the whole of the quarry is looking at it. Nobody asks you for it. You see Tarrax's eyes, once, go to your belt, and come back.
  $ b1.senate_ruling = "deferred"
  $ b1.tarrax_after = "wary"
  $ standing += 1
  $ bond.tarrax += 1
?else
  You take the pegs out of your sleeve, and the General looks at them, and then at you, and then he laughs, quite softly. "Oh, little one," he says. "It is a very thin opinion." But he goes, and he sits down in the mouth of the old green door, with his back to the stair, and lets you drive them in, one at a time, into the bronze, with the heel of your hand.
  The frost runs out across the metal in thin white lines, like ice across a pond. It holds. It looks as though it would hold for a very long time. It does not, you know, look like it would hold for ever.
  The Lictors stand in two red files and watch it, and nobody says that it is not enough. You see the First's hood turn. You see Tarrax's face. He has the look of a man who has been given a bill that he cannot pay, and has been told that it is a kindness.
