He does not look back. It is the last thing that you see of him, a very big man in a plain grey cloak with a bronze flower at the throat, in the torchlight, with a straight back. And the sound that follows him up the aisle is the sound of the grey line, going down on its knees, one by one, in the dust.
-> dawn

# dawn
## The grey hour
It is not like any dawn that you have seen. There is no ceremony in it. The torches have gone out, and the quarry is a pale scooped hollow of stone with the sky coming up behind it, and the lines have gone, one by one, without a word, like water into sand.
Prisca is sitting on the edge of the aisle with her abacus in her lap. She has not moved the beads. She says, as you come up, "I have counted." She looks at the quarry floor, and at the empty door, and at the swept stone with its long smear of torch smoke. "It is not a small sum." She says it in the voice of a woman reading a lease. "I should like you to know that I would pay it again."
?if b1.senate_ruling == "accepted"
  The Senate has gone back to its rooms with a plain look of having been, for once, correct, and of not at all enjoying it. There will be a good many documents about it. The Merchant house, you suspect, will be asked to hold some of them.
?elif b1.senate_ruling == "deferred"
  The Senate has gone back to its rooms with a plain look of having been forced, for once, to think. It will not say so. It will be very civil to you, and to Meritamun, for some months, and it will not be a thing that you will be able to enjoy.
?else
  The Senate has gone back to its rooms, and it is not a plain look that it has. It is the look of an immense old machine that has been asked to do a thing for which it was not built, and has done it, and is now very slowly, very precisely, beginning to take note.
?end
Meritamun comes up the cliff path in the first grey, with her stylus behind her ear and the white linen folded over her arm. She stops beside you. She looks at the open door of the bronze.
?if original("general", "fate") == "sealed"
  "I have not written it down," she says. "I should like you to know that I was asked not to, and that I find that I am going to obey."
?elif original("general", "fate") == "drunk"
  "I shall record it," says Meritamun, very quietly, "in a form that nobody will be able to read for two hundred years. I find that I am a good deal more frightened of what you have become than of what you did." She does not say it unkindly. She says it as a scholar says what is in front of her.
?else
  "I shall record it," says Meritamun, "as I recorded the rest. He did not ask me not to. He asked that I put in the part about the hat."
?end
She goes. You stand at the lip of the old quarry for some time, in the cold, with the sun coming. It is not a thing that you can describe. It is the sense of having done something that cannot be undone and having not yet found out what it was.
?if hunger > 70
  You are hungry. You are more than hungry. You are going to be going on like this for a long time.
?end
Somewhere below, in the new white streets, a line of workmen is going down to the rebuilding, with their tools on their shoulders, and a boy is whistling.
You think, with the cold perfect clarity of the hour before the sun, that Rome is going to have to be left. It is not a thing that you have decided. It is only a thing that you know, as one knows that it is going to rain. There is a long dark ahead, and a house in the hills, and a great many people who will need to be provided for when you are not there.
$ do rest()
=> next
